davisd@auburn.ee.washington.edu writes:
> 
> > From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
> >
> > ... Now imagine you were looking at an area [ of neurons ]
> > being trained to recognize crows. The neurons in this area might
> > bounce around wildly, but each time the person sees a black crow, the
> > the value will be pushed towards a stable equilibrium forcing the person
> > to believe with high confidence that the next crow he will see is black.
> 
> This kind of analysis leads one into conceptual errors pretty quickly.
> Neurons are not "being trained to recognize crows". They are part of a
> self organizing system without predetermined structural relationship
> to the rest of the world.

   That was not the point of my example. Of course there are no specific
neurons in the brain whose job it is to learn to recognize crows. The point
is, some abstract structure forms a self-organizing class around
crowness. Whether it is neurons, or some higher level structure, it doesn't
matter. Don't take things too literally.

> Further, what meaning can we attach to "believe with high confidence
> the next crow he will see is black"? By whose category of crow are we
> going to make this judgment?  Lets take it from the viewpoint of our
> crow watcher. He has abstracted from specific birds he has seen to a
> concept, and has labelled that concept crow. He now sees a white bird
> that has a lot of the characteristics that his crow abstraction has.
> If his crow abstraction included color as a characteristic, he will
> probably be surprised at this white bird, and may revise his crow
> abstraction. If not, he will just see another crow.

   You're missing the point. After he sees enough black crows, he will
associate blackness and crows. If he starts seeing a lot of white
crows, he will create two new classes of crows, say "Northern 
White Crow" and "Southern Black Crow"  And maintain a general class
of crows which includes both black and white. The point is,
if he NEVER sees a white crow, blackness will become a 
crow property and if asked what characteristics make a crow, he will
include black.

   Most people have never seen a white albino moose, so most people think 
mooses are brown and believe strongly in that assessment.
 
> In short, our bird watcher is not attempting to match his concept of
> crow to a crow Ideal, he is grouping his experience. Depending on how
> he has grouped his experience, a bird with a lot of the
> characteristics of what we call crows, except that it is white, may or
> may not surprise him.

   Your hypothetical birder watcher doesn't exist in our reality. Most
people think crows are black because most crows they see are black. If
someone were to see a white crow, they would probably put it in another
class. 

   You agreed with my point anyway. My post provided an example of grouped
behavior. The more you see something, the more likely you are to create
a new category for it. Once that category is matured, one will tend to
believe that category as objective fact. If something doesn't fit into
the category after it is matured, a new category is created.

   I simply started out with the crow category already created ("assume
there is a group of neurons...") and then examined the behavior in
terms of a belief threshold. One these categories mature, they lose
their plasticity and the birdwatcher becomes closed-mined. Only
repeatable, solid evidence will convince him to revise his definition
of crows. Hearsay probably won't.

-Ray


