Perhaps I'm misunderstanding you, but you still seem to exhibit the
tendencies which I find bothersome.

There is no ideal crowness around which the self-organizing class
forms itself. The class forms according to the accumulated experience
and motivations of our crow watcher.

>    You're missing the point. After he sees enough black crows, he will
> associate blackness and crows. If he starts seeing a lot of white
> crows, he will create two new classes of crows, say "Northern 
> White Crow" and "Southern Black Crow"  And maintain a general class
> of crows which includes both black and white. The point is,
> if he NEVER sees a white crow, blackness will become a 
> crow property and if asked what characteristics make a crow, he will
> include black.

Because color is a characteristic used by most people when they form
grouping of material objects, it is likely that blackness will be part
of anyone's concept of crowness. I had assumed you were trying to make
a general argument, however, in which any characteristic could be
substituted for color. A lot of characteristics are ignored when we
organize our experiences, and so the general argument does not hold.

>    You agreed with my point anyway. My post provided an example of grouped
> behavior. The more you see something, the more likely you are to create
> a new category for it. 

This again appears to exhibit the conceptual mistake which I am trying
to point out.

When you say "the more you see something", it appears to me that you
have already assumed a pre-existing category of crowness that makes
the black birds you see instances of the something you are seeing more
of. Otherwise, the black birds would simply be instances of different
things. 

Whenever I talk about the black birds you see, I am of course
characterizing them as some group so that I can refer to them. If that
is all you are doing when you say crows, then that is fine. However, I
object to reasoning about the formation of categories which sneaks in
the categories as preset concepts through this verbal backdoor. This
kind of reasoning accounts for a lot of misconceptions and meaningless
argument, which is what I've tried to point out.

