From: martino@gomez.jpl.nasa.gov (Martin R. Olah)
Date: Mon, 31 Jan 1994 16:28:00 -0800
Subject: [#94-1-750] ECON: Solar Panels

When I was a silicon crystal grower I was amazed at the effort put into
making such inefficient power sources.  The furnaces run in the 85-100 Kw range
most of the time, up to a max of 130Kw. If you lose structure you generally
melt the ingot back and start over. If you get a good crystal, half gets wasted
when cutting. Of course plent of nasty chemicals get used for cleaning the 
wafers, like trichloroethelene. Further down the line are plenty of other 
processes of etching and cleaning using various chemicals most   
environmentalists wouldn't be to crazy about.
  
  Polycrystaline wafers are less efficient but cheaper because you don't have
to worry about maintaining the proper molecular orientation, something that can
be quite difficult. Contamination, speed/power fluctuations etc. don't have
much negative effect once a crystal no longer has a set orientation.     
  
  Many calculators use amorphous cells, of which I have no experience or inside
knowledge, but they are certainly the least effecient.
  
  The reason everyone doesn't use photovoltaics is not due to some conspiracy.
They are expensive and efficiency is usually much less than 20%. Using 
materials other than silicon can boost efficiency, but the price also.
Many schemes have and will be attempted, but for the forseeable future PV's
will only make sense when grid independance is the primary consideration.
Sorry I didn't have any hard figures to add to the discusion.

                               O Martin

P.S. My father is about to help set up a PV plant in China, so we'll see what
THEY can do to the price somewhere down the line. The only advantage I can see 
them having is in labor costs, but PV's biggest expense is material and energy
input. We'll see.


