From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Date: Mon, 31 Jan 94 20:31:20 EST
Subject: [#94-1-755] META: Will we ever get along?

Dan Davis says-

> I've come to the opposite conclusion. I think the list is becoming
> more stable in its core members. Those who have axes to grind will
> grind and grind until they exhaust themselves. Then they leave. Those
> who remain after that point likely no longer have axes to grind, and
> instead read the list to learn and share what they have learned. They
> no longer must convert the infidels, they just put out their ideas and
> discuss them with those who respond in interesting ways. 

A similar experience to compare: I was on the paper list "The [Libertarian]
Connection" for over 10 years, and the same pattern emerged.  There are
flamers I called "comets" who burn brightly for a short time and then
leave.  Also, there are people who write strong and out-of-the-ordinary
stuff for a longer time, but don't like the environment, have quarrels
with the proprietor, or have more pressing things to do, and leave after 
a couple years.  Some people come in with heavy axes to grind and grind
them for a couple years and then either leave or mellow out.  (TC had
no rules about content but the equilibrium still maintained itself.)

To me it's saddest when someone leaves whose viewpoint is both unusual 
for the list, and who is well spoken.  Like Tim May was (sometimes!).
But, that's the way they go.

I went through pretty intense paranoias about The
Connection for *years*.  Some of these people who were disagreeing with
me...I couldn't believe how diabolical they were.  It was a constant
eye-blinking, head-shaking effort to write reasonable replies (and we
had about a month to write them).  So I have no problem believing that 
some people find this list unpleasant.

I think our memes are not evolved for this kind of conversation yet.
My own expectations (at least) about people's behavior and reactions 
(including my own) are constantly upset.  We think we understand
conversation, but I think we've been relying on methods that worked
for reasons we are only slowly understanding.  But I love that kind of
self-discovery, archeology of our programming.

-fnerd
