Subject: [#94-1-760] ECON: Solar Panels

  Ok, a did a little research, apparently the claims I made about PV
only apply to amorphous cells not the polycrystalline type (more expensive,
but last 10+ years)

  Amorphous cells suffer from low conversion efficiency, rapid power drop
off with age (1/2 power in 3-4 years), and delamination because of the
environment.

  Still, given Dani's $380 12 year panels, they still aren't competitive
with grid power. 70m^2/0.44m^2/panel*$380/panel=~$60,000 to outfit the
roof of your house. Now the expected lifetime is 12 years. I use
more power than the average, but my typical bill is $100/month. In
12 years, this is only ~$14,000, so PV must come down by a factor of 4
just to break even with grid power. This doesn't take into account
the maintainence required for PV. They must be cleaned, especially on
the north east coast during winter and spring. If I had PV installed, right 
now it would be frozen over with about 3 layers of ice, and my roof
is fairly inaccessible. Many people can't justify such a huge initial
investment to switch over their power source, although I could see
PV being incorporated into new houses.

  As for centralized PV, the environmentalists don't like it. There is 
concern that PV farms will screw up ecosystems. Power companies can't
justify it either since the RoI is too low.

  Conclusion: PV has a way to go before it is economically competitive
with alternative sources.

